  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  # Skia's version is its Chrome milestone (SK_MILESTONE in\n  # include/core/SkMilestone.h), which is also its unit of ABI compatibility.\n  version: \"143.0.0\"\n  # Skia publishes no usable tags: the only chrome/m* tags are m37-m39 from 2014,\n  # and canvaskit/* tags version the CanvasKit WASM product rather than Skia.\n  # Milestones are branches, which cannot be used as a source because the archive\n  # (and its hash) changes whenever the branch moves. So we pin a commit: this is\n  # the final commit of chrome/m143, which upstream has since retired, so the\n  # branch will not move again. m143 is the milestone skia-pathops builds against.\n  commit: b88b661b7fca3a45cf6d5d684d1259939eca1c71\n\nrecipe:\n  name: skia\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/google/skia/archive/${{ commit }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 6429d5d4869e8e48a076ff22daa15461b2bdc338dd92dcf3dc56a44dcc5d2b49\n  patches:\n    - patches/0001-link-pathops-into-the-skia-component.patch\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n\noutputs:\n  - package:\n      name: libskia\n    build:\n      script:\n        # one script for every platform, so the unix and Windows paths cannot drift\n        file: build_libskia.nu\n      variant:\n        ignore_keys:\n          # python is only here because GN needs it; nothing Python-specific ends\n          # up in the library, so it must not turn a plain C++ library into one\n          # build per Python version\n          - python\n    requirements:\n      build:\n        - ${{ compiler('cxx') }}\n        - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n        - gn\n        - ninja\n        - nushell\n        # GN runs python for exec_script() and for its action() targets\n        - python\n      run_exports:\n        # Skia has no stable ABI and no versioned soname; every milestone is a\n        # fresh ABI, so pin consumers to the milestone they were built against.\n        - ${{ pin_subpackage('libskia', upper_bound='x') }}\n    tests:\n      - package_contents:\n          lib:\n            - skia\n          include:\n            - skia/include/core/SkPath.h\n            - skia/include/pathops/SkPathOps.h\n          files:\n            - ${{ \"Library/\" if win }}lib/pkgconfig/skia.pc\n      - script:\n          - if: unix\n            then:\n              - pkg-config --print-errors --exists skia\n              - ${CXX} ${CXXFLAGS} -std=c++17 test_skia.cpp $(pkg-config --cflags --libs skia) ${LDFLAGS} -o test_skia\n              - ./test_skia\n            else:\n              - cl /std:c++17 /EHsc /I\"%LIBRARY_PREFIX%\\include\\skia\" test_skia.cpp /link /LIBPATH:\"%LIBRARY_PREFIX%\\lib\" skia.lib\n              - test_skia.exe\n        files:\n          recipe:\n            - test_skia.cpp\n        requirements:\n          build:\n            - ${{ compiler('cxx') }}\n            - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n            - if: unix\n              then: pkg-config\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://skia.org\n  repository: https://github.com/google/skia\n  documentation: https://skia.org/docs/\n  license: BSD-3-Clause\n  license_file: LICENSE\n  summary: Skia is a complete 2D graphic library for drawing text, geometries and images\n  description: |\n    Skia is an open source 2D graphics library which provides common APIs that\n    work across a variety of hardware and software platforms. It serves as the\n    graphics engine for Google Chrome and ChromeOS, Android, Flutter and many\n    other products.\n\n    This build is deliberately minimal: it provides Skia's geometry and path\n    handling (including pathops) with no GPU backends, image codecs, text shaping\n    or system font managers, which is what allows it to be built without Skia's\n    third_party/externals checkout.\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - pavelzw\n",
